.docname {Notification}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Breach | A value outside its limit | 31 °C, limit 28 °C |
    | Alert | What a person receives about a breach | "Room 2 is too hot" |
    | Cooldown | A pause during which repeats of one alert are muted | 5 minutes per room and field |
    | Web Push | Browser notifications that arrive with no tab open | Phone lock screen |
    | Push subscription | One device's address for Web Push | Ada's laptop |
    | Dead-letter topic | Where messages that cannot be handled wait | `alerts.dlq` |
    | System caller | A service calling with no person behind it | `system:notification-service` |

Realises the [Alerting](../domain-model/domain-alerting.qd) domain. Rust, MongoDB, Redis. Code-level page: [Notification Service](../services/notification.qd).

Decides who should hear about a breach, then tells them twice: in the app and by Web Push.

---

## Who talks to it

.mermaid
    flowchart LR
        T["telemetry"] == "breaches" ==> N["notification"]
        Tw["digital-twin"] -- "upload failed" --> N
        C["client"] -- "devices, choices, test alert" --> N
        N -- "who owns this building?" --> Tw
        N -. "in-app alert" .-> S["socket"]
        N -- "Web Push" --> P["Push provider"]

Thick: Kafka. Thin: a request. Dotted: Redis.

Two things it keeps, kept apart:

| Kept | Holds | Why apart |
|---|---|---|
| Devices | Where each person can be reached | A lost phone is removed without losing choices |
| Choices | What each person wants to hear | Choices change without registering devices again |

---

## Inside

.mermaid
    flowchart LR
        subgraph In["Driving adapters"]
            H["HTTP"]
            K["Kafka: alerts"]
        end
        subgraph Core["Use cases"]
            AL["Alerts: mute repeats, find owners, send"]
            PU["Push: pick devices, send, prune"]
            PR["Devices and choices"]
        end
        subgraph Out["Driven adapters"]
            DB[("MongoDB")]
            R["Redis: cooldown, in-app channel"]
            TW["digital-twin lookup"]
            WP["Web Push"]
        end
        H --> AL
        H --> PR
        K --> AL
        AL --> PU
        AL -.-> R & TW
        PU -.-> DB & WP
        PR -.-> DB

Two ways in, one alert logic. A test alert and a real breach go through the same steps.

---

## A breach becomes an alert

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        In["Breach from Kafka"] --> M{"Muted? Same room and field in the last 5 min"}
        M -- "yes" --> Stop["Done, nothing sent"]
        M -- "no" --> L{"Who owns the building?"}
        L -- "lookup failed" --> DLQ["Parked on alerts.dlq, not muted"]
        L -- "no owner" --> Drop["Dropped, logged, muted"]
        L -- "owners" --> Send["In-app and Web Push to each owner organization"]
        Send --> Mute["Muted for 5 min"]

| Case | Why |
|---|---|
| Lookup failed: not muted | The alert can be sent again once `digital-twin` is back |
| No owner: muted | Every following tick would flood the log otherwise |
| Never sent with no organization | It would reach every browser of every customer |

**Example.** Room 2 reads 31 °C every 10 s, limit 28 °C. The first breach alerts at 10:00:00. The next 29 are muted. The one at 10:05:00 alerts again.

Watch the "failed" and "unroutable" counts: either means an alert reached nobody.

---

## Finding the owners

- `notification` asks `digital-twin` and keeps no copy of buildings.
- The lookup gives up after 2 s. A stuck `digital-twin` must not hold every alert behind it.
- Answers are kept 15 minutes. An empty answer or a failure is never kept, so a building that just finished registering is found on the next alert.
- **Why 15 minutes and not more:** a building moved to another organization keeps alerting the old one until the entry expires. Longer saves almost nothing, since the cooldown already limits lookups.

`socket` keeps the same answer only 60 s. Different question: it decides who may read live data, so it needs to be fresher.

---

## Who may receive

| Caller | Gets alerts for |
|---|---|
| A person, through the test route | Owner organizations they belong to, nothing else |
| A system caller | Every owner organization |

A system caller has no memberships. Filtering would drop everything, and the failed-upload alert from `digital-twin` would vanish. Only services inside the mesh can be a system caller: the edge strips the claims header from outside requests.

The system identity keeps an older name, `system:notification-service`, on purpose. It is checked byte for byte at a trust boundary.

---

## Web Push

- A device the push provider calls gone, with `410` or `403`, is removed on the next send. No cleanup job needed.

---

## Gaps

- **`alerts.dlq` has no reader.** Parked alerts wait there until someone replays them by hand.
- **Code vs domain:** a long breach alerts every 5 minutes, not once. See [Telemetry](telemetry-architecture.qd).
